# Task 3 - the same measurements, read again and again.
#
# k-means repeats one step until it settles. Every round assigns each
# record to its nearest centroid, then recomputes each centroid from the
# records that chose it. The assignment is per record and needs no
# coordination; the recomputation is per centroid and needs all of them.
#
# showering.csv is one shower per line:
#     start_hour,volume_dl,temperature_c,duration_s
# Four people used this shower, and each used it differently. Whether the
# clusters find them is the question.
#
# The rounds below are written out one after the other on purpose. That is
# what an iterative job is: the same pass over the same data, again.
# `cache` is the one line that stops each round starting over.
#
# Note how the centroid is recomputed. A mean is a sum divided by a count,
# so each record carries both and the reducer adds them. Writing it as a
# running average instead — reduceByKey(lambda a, b: (a + b) / 2) — gives a
# different answer depending on how the rows were split across machines,
# and Spark does not promise you any particular split.

@machine
class Executor:
    pass

e1 = Executor(speed=1.0)
# step 5: add   error_rate=0.3, on_crash="restart", restart_after=0.5
e2 = Executor(speed=1.0)
e3 = Executor(speed=1.0)

# --- the world -------------------------------------------------------

world = World(machines=[e1, e2, e3])

# --- the pipeline ----------------------------------------------------

showers = textFile("showering.csv")
fields = showers.map(lambda row: row.split(","))
# A shower that used less than 50 dL was the tap being run, not a shower.
real = fields.filter(lambda f: float(f[1]) >= 50.0)
kept = real.cache()          # step 2: make this   kept = real.map(lambda f: f)

# round one: four starting centroids, picked by hand, as volume in dL
assigned1 = kept.map(lambda f: (min(range(4), key=lambda i: abs(float(f[1]) - [60, 130, 200, 280][i])), (float(f[1]), 1)))
sums1 = assigned1.reduceByKey(lambda a, b: (a[0] + b[0], a[1] + b[1]))
centroids1 = sums1.mapValues(lambda s: s[0] / s[1])

# round two: the same pass, with the centroids round one produced.
# step 3: run it, read centroids1 off the diagram, and put those four
#         numbers in the list below in place of the starting ones.
assigned2 = kept.map(lambda f: (min(range(4), key=lambda i: abs(float(f[1]) - [60, 130, 200, 280][i])), (float(f[1]), 1)))
sums2 = assigned2.reduceByKey(lambda a, b: (a[0] + b[0], a[1] + b[1]))
centroids2 = sums2.mapValues(lambda s: s[0] / s[1])

# step 4: add a third round by copying the three lines above and renaming
#         them, and say what one more round costs.

job = Spark(pipeline=centroids2, lose=kept)

world.run(job)
